## Finding C: c_emit_lower.oo split-heuristic falsification (2026-09-01)

The CAP_BOUNDARY.oot heuristic for c_emit_lower.oo said: "split into c_emit_fs.oo (read/write/path_exists/file_size) + c_emit_alloc.oo (alloc_bytes/free/realloc) + keep c_emit_lower.oo as dispatcher".

Attempted this turn: extracted the 4 fs op branches (read_file, write_file, path_exists, file_size) into a helper function c_emit_dispatch_fs_op. Result: file grew from 255 to **264 lines (8 over 256 cap)**. The helper function body was the same length as the inline branches, and there was only one call site, so the extraction was net-positive on lines.

Reverted immediately. File is back to 255 lines, under 256 cap, 4 fs op error messages intact.

The lesson: the CAP_BOUNDARY.oot heuristic for c_emit_lower.oo is INCORRECT for partial extraction. To actually split this file, you need to extract MULTIPLE op families (fs + alloc + time + rand + sys) into separate files so the dispatcher in c_emit_lower.oo shrinks significantly. A single-family extraction just shifts the code around.

Correct split heuristic: extract fs (4 ops) + alloc (3 ops) + time (2 ops) + rand (2 ops) + sys (2 ops) = 13 op branches into 5 sibling files. The dispatcher in c_emit_lower.oo would shrink by ~80 lines (from 237 to ~157), giving a healthy margin. This is a multi-file refactor that requires careful coordination with the existing imports and the libfloor_call layer. Deferred.
